  "description": "Issue #830, execution twin of `heritage/check/heritage-value-of-key-reader-in-parentheses`. `execute()` runs `parse()` and never `check()` (the harness `produce()` also returns under `check: true` before the `execute` branch), so the runtime holds its own copy of these rules and the parenthesized reader must be proven to RUN, not merely to parse clean. Every print pins a VALUE that exists only if the `(` path actually entered the `value-of-reader` (`spec/grammar.md:203`, defined :217) instead of taking `value` as a `parenthesized-call` callee (:215) — the defect #830 reported, fixed incidentally by #885 and pinned here. This twin executes the SAME shape set its check twin pins, so no shape is proven at parse-time only: the reader wholly parenthesized, as its own dictionary operand, as its own key operand (the inner reader reports the word \"a\", which the outer then uses as its key — a silent re-parse as a call could not yield 7), redundantly grouped, infix `+` on either side, `.field` and `[selector]` postfixes (proving it is a real `primary`, :192), and both readers parenthesized. The last two prints cross-check the Heritage reader against the Core `:d[\"a\"]`/`:nested.inner.x` twins it corresponds to, in the same run. Values are pinned rather than wording because the harness excludes `message` from comparison. The reader operates on a dict, so Heritage also needs Data (`spec/conformance.md:305`).",
